text:
Model a command and a rule 
 Start with a sentence from the domain: “Add a positive amount to this counter.” This gives you a command ( AddToCounter ), one validation rule (amount greater than zero), and an event ( CountAdded ) for the accepted result. A rejected request throws and writes no event in this example. On this page Express the decision in C Extend the model 

 Express the decision in C 
 This example uses the current Troolio.Core 10.0.8 actor API. Put the code in a .NET 10 project with Troolio.Core and Microsoft.Orleans.Sdk references. 
 using Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration;
using Orleans;
using Troolio.Core;
using Troolio.Stores;

public interface IValidatedCounterActor : IActor { }

[GenerateSerializer]
public record AddToCounter(Metadata Headers, int Amount)
    : Command<IValidatedCounterActor>(Headers);

[GenerateSerializer]
public record CountAdded(Metadata Headers, int Amount) : Event(Headers);

[GenerateSerializer]
public record GetValidatedCount : Query<IValidatedCounterActor, int>;

public sealed class ValidatedCounterActor(IStore store, IConfiguration configuration)
    : EventSourcedActor(store, configuration), IValidatedCounterActor
{
    private int count;

    public IEnumerable<Event> Handle(AddToCounter command)
    {
        if (command.Amount <= 0)
            throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(command.Amount));
        return [new CountAdded(command.Headers, command.Amount)];
    }

    public void On(CountAdded @event) => count += @event.Amount;
    public int Handle(GetValidatedCount _) => count;
}

 The command handler checks a business rule before producing an event.  On  only changes in-memory state; the configured store keeps the event history. During replay, applying the same  CountAdded  events reconstructs the same count. 
 Extend the model 
 A larger feature may produce several events or involve another actor. Name events in the past tense, keep the payload sufficient to replay state, and decide explicitly whether a rejection is an exception or a recorded domain event. Test both branches and a replay of historical events before changing event formats. 
 Continue with  first actor ,  events and state , then  projections .

Build your first actor 
 An event-sourced actor turns a command into an event and rebuilds its in-memory state by applying events. This counter is deliberately small so the roles are visible. The example uses Troolio.Core 10.0.8 and Orleans 10. On this page Define messages and the actor Compile the example Next decisions 

 Define messages and the actor 
 using Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration;
using Orleans;
using Troolio.Core;
using Troolio.Stores;

namespace MyApp;

public interface ICounterActor : IActor { }

[GenerateSerializer]
public record Increment(Metadata Headers) : Command<ICounterActor>(Headers);

[GenerateSerializer]
public record Incremented(Metadata Headers) : Event(Headers);

[GenerateSerializer]
public record CurrentCount : Query<ICounterActor, int>;

public sealed class CounterActor(IStore store, IConfiguration configuration)
    : EventSourcedActor(store, configuration), ICounterActor
{
    private int count;

    public IEnumerable<Event> Handle(Increment command) =>
        [new Incremented(command.Headers)];

    public void On(Incremented _) => count++;

    public int Handle(CurrentCount _) => count;
}

 Increment  is a command.  Handle  emits an  Incremented  event.  On  applies the event both after an accepted append and during replay when the actor activates again.  CurrentCount  reads the actor's current state. The actor's event stream, not the  count  field, is the durable write history when a durable store is configured. 
 Metadata  carries correlation, user, device, and message IDs. In a serving application, the host must bind the user and target to the current authenticated principal. Caller supplied headers alone are not authority. 
 Compile the example 
 Add the snippet as  Counter.cs  in a  net10.0  class library that references  Troolio.Core  10.0.8 and  Microsoft.Orleans.Sdk  10.0.0, then run: 
 dotnet build -c Release

 The snippet describes actor behavior inside a host.  Host setup  shows the next wiring step. 
 Next decisions 

 Choose an  event store and serializer . The bundled in-memory store is for local learning and tests. 
 Choose how to  project events into read models . 
 Decide whether the ordinary command path is sufficient or the  selected atomic route  is required.

Build a workshop workflow 
 A counter shows the actor API. A workshop registration shows how the pieces fit in an application. This example lets an attendee request a registration, records it in a workshop roster, projects a registration card to SQL, and offers a scoped reader for the UI. On this page Try the code 1. Give each actor a responsibility 2. Send intent with trusted context 3. Validate and record the outcome 4. React with another actor command 5. Project a card for readers 6. Read with the caller's access scope 7. Add effects through durable work 
 The downloadable  workshop example  contains the original messages, actors, listeners, read model, SQL writer, schema, and application helpers. It is a .NET 10 class library of application building blocks. Configure a host, provider credentials, migrations, and subscriptions before serving it; it does not include a Web API or hosted notification provider. 

 Try the code 
 Clone the public docs repository and build the example with the .NET 10 SDK: 
 git clone https://github.com/Fifty3North/troolio-docs.git
cd troolio-docs
dotnet build examples/workshop/Workshop.csproj -c Release

 The SQL read path uses  RegistrationCardsProjection  and  SqlRegistrationCards . The optional linked  RegistrationDetails  view illustrates the resolver API and requires ordered, deduplicated source delivery. Start with the  host guide  to register the assemblies and application services for your chosen path. 
 1. Give each actor a responsibility 
 A registration actor owns one registration's lifecycle. A workshop roster actor owns the registration IDs recorded for one workshop. The UI reads small cards from SQL instead of asking every registration actor for its state. 
 This example records a request. A finite seat-capacity invariant would need its own authoritative decision in the workshop actor or a selected multi-participant route. The two-role example alone does not guarantee a seat reservation across stores. 
 2. Send intent with trusted context 
 The application allocates a stable registration ID and obtains the user and device from its authenticated boundary. That ID is also the actor key. 
 public static Task RequestAsync(ITroolioClient client, Guid registrationId, Guid workshopId,
    string displayName, Guid authenticatedUserId, Guid verifiedDeviceId, Guid correlationId)
{
    Metadata headers = MessageContext.AtIngress(correlationId, authenticatedUserId, verifiedDeviceId);
    return client.Tell<IRegistrationActor>(registrationId.ToString("D"),
        new RequestRegistration(headers, registrationId, workshopId, displayName));
}

 Keep correlation across the related interaction. Ordinary Troolio dispatch supplies transaction and message relationships as described in  metadata . A retry must still be reconciled against the registration's state and the selected route's operation identity when that route is used. 
 3. Validate and record the outcome 
 The registration actor delegates decisions to pure domain rules and state changes to event application: 
 public sealed class RegistrationActor : EventSourcedActor<RegistrationState>, IRegistrationActor
{
    public RegistrationActor(IStore store, IConfiguration configuration) : base(store, configuration)
        => State = RegistrationState.Empty;

    public IEnumerable<Event> Handle(RequestRegistration command)
    {
        if (!Guid.TryParse(Id, out Guid actorId) || actorId != command.RegistrationId)
            throw new ArgumentException("The actor key must match the registration ID.");
        return RegistrationRules.Request(State, command);
    }

    public IEnumerable<Event> Handle(CancelRegistration command) => RegistrationRules.Cancel(State, command);
    public void On(RegistrationRequested e) => State = State.Apply(e);
    public void On(RegistrationCancelled e) => State = State.Apply(e);
    public RegistrationState Handle(GetRegistration _) => State;
}

 A valid request produces  RegistrationRequested ; cancellation produces  RegistrationCancelled . The state stores the registration, workshop, attendee, display name, and status. A duplicate request with identical business details returns no new domain event in the ordinary sample; a conflicting reuse of the registration ID is rejected. Selected no-op completion has its own optional capability and is not implied by this example. 
 4. React with another actor command 
 The orchestration listener forwards the event's headers and addresses the workshop roster using the workshop ID carried by the event. 
 [OrchestrationStreamSubscription(typeof(RegistrationActor))]
public sealed class WorkshopEnrolmentOrchestration : OrchestrationActor
{
    public Task On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationRequested> envelope) =>
        System.ActorOf<IWorkshopRosterActor>(envelope.Event.WorkshopId.ToString("D"))
            .Tell(new AddAttendee(envelope.Event.Headers, envelope.Event.WorkshopId,
                envelope.Event.RegistrationId, envelope.Event.AttendeeId));

    public Task On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationCancelled> envelope) =>
        System.ActorOf<IWorkshopRosterActor>(envelope.Event.WorkshopId.ToString("D"))
            .Tell(new RemoveAttendee(envelope.Event.Headers, envelope.Event.WorkshopId,
                envelope.Event.RegistrationId));
}

 The roster stores registration IDs rather than incrementing an unqualified count. A repeated add can therefore be recognized. It also records removal tombstones so a late add after cancellation cannot resurrect the entry. It returns the  Tell  task so the listener's work remains observable to the caller's protocol. 
 This is ordinary event-to-command orchestration. For a selected atomic workflow, register the reviewed planning handlers and durable closure/store contracts described in  orchestration  and  atomic commit . 
 5. Project a card for readers 
 A separate listener turns a committed registration event into the view needed by the UI: 
 [ProjectionStreamSubscription(typeof(RegistrationActor))]
public sealed class RegistrationCardsProjection(IRegistrationCardWriter writer) : ProjectionActor
{
    public Task On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationRequested> envelope)
    {
        RegistrationCard card = RegistrationCards.From(envelope.Event);
        return writer.ApplyAsync(card, RegistrationCards.Source(envelope, card), CancellationToken.None);
    }

    public Task On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationCancelled> envelope)
    {
        RegistrationCard card = RegistrationCards.From(envelope.Event);
        return writer.ApplyAsync(card, RegistrationCards.Source(envelope, card), CancellationToken.None);
    }
}

 The SQL writer atomically records its source identity and replaces the card only when an appropriate newer source snapshot arrives. It rejects a changed domain payload for a retained source identity. Both event variants carry the fields needed for the card, so this teaching model can ignore an older full snapshot safely. Delta-based projections need stricter ordering and gap recovery. 
 Apply  schema.sql  through the application's migration process and register  IRegistrationCardWriter  with the host. This writer is an application read-model implementation; it is separate from the framework's selected event transaction store. 
 6. Read with the caller's access scope 
 SqlRegistrationCards.GetForUserAsync  queries one registration ID and the current authenticated attendee ID in SQL. Unauthorized and absent rows both yield no card. A staff view would need a separate reviewed authorization rule and bounded query. 
 A command can finish before the card catches up. Represent that state honestly in the UI, and use  realtime refresh hints  to trigger another authorized read after the projection commits. 
 7. Add effects through durable work 
 Creating a notification fact in a projection is different from sending a confirmation through a provider. Keep the provider request behind durable intent state, a stable idempotency key, and recovery. Replaying the registration actor or rebuilding the SQL card should not resend confirmations. 
 Follow  notifications and background work  for that design. Then study  projection design ,  read models , and  idempotency  before extending this example.

Configure a host 
 A Troolio host composes Microsoft Orleans with the actor assembly, event store, serializer, and application services. The basic  TroolioServer  overload shown here is a starting point for local evaluation with Troolio.Core 10.0.8. On this page Register actors and storage Choose a real deployment profile Serving contract 
 Register actors and storage 
 using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Troolio.Core;
using Troolio.Core.Serialization;
using Troolio.Stores;

namespace MyApp;

public static class CounterHost
{
    public static IHost Build() => Host.CreateDefaultBuilder()
        .TroolioServer<InMemoryStore, JsonEventSerializer>(
            appName: "Counter",
            registerAssemblies: [typeof(CounterActor).Assembly],
            configureDelegates: _ => { },
            builderDelegates: null,
            disableActors: null);
}

 registerAssemblies  tells the host where to discover actor and projection types. The generic parameters choose an  IStore  and an  IEventSerializer .  configureDelegates  is where the application registers its own services.  TroolioServer  builds an  IHost ; the caller still starts and stops it according to the application's lifetime. 
 Choose a real deployment profile 
 The basic overload retains the bundled Local/Azure profile. An overload with  configureClustering  gives the application control over Orleans membership, endpoints, storage, reminders, and streams. When you use that callback, supply  all  required Orleans services for your deployment. This callback does not replace the domain event store or historical replay. 
 The in-memory store does not survive process exit. For persistent operation, choose a compatible adapter and review its schema, credentials, transaction contract, projection workers, and recovery path. See  SQL Server ,  KurrentDB , and the  combined integration . 
 Serving contract 
 A host must provide current caller authentication and command authorization, trusted source and owner observations where needed, route authority, provider idempotency, retention windows, and recovery operations. A package reference or successful silo startup does not establish serving readiness. Use the  deployment checklist  to review these pieces together.

Projections and read models 
 A projection transforms events into a useful view. A read model is the result a query uses. In a workshop application, a registration actor owns its lifecycle, a projection writes a registration card to SQL, and an authorized reader returns that card to the attendee. On this page Three roles in one application A typed projection listener A small immutable read model Authorization and freshness 
 This lets the command side focus on decisions while the query side has fields and indexes suited to the page. 

 Three roles in one application 



 Role 
 Responsibility 
 Example 




 Event-sourced actor 
 Validate a command and record domain events. 
 Registration lifecycle. 


 Projection 
 Translate delivered source events into another data shape. 
 SQL registration card writer. 


 Read model and reader 
 Answer the page's question under current access rules. 
 One attendee's registration card. 



 An actor query reads that actor's current reconstructed state. A cross-actor list should usually read an application query model rather than activating every actor in the list.  CQRS in practice  explains that choice. 
 A typed projection listener 
 The example subscribes to registration events and delegates the database work to an application writer. 
 [ProjectionStreamSubscription(typeof(RegistrationActor))]
public sealed class RegistrationCardsProjection(IRegistrationCardWriter writer) : ProjectionActor
{
    public Task On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationRequested> envelope)
    {
        RegistrationCard card = RegistrationCards.From(envelope.Event);
        return writer.ApplyAsync(card, RegistrationCards.Source(envelope, card), CancellationToken.None);
    }

    public Task On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationCancelled> envelope)
    {
        RegistrationCard card = RegistrationCards.From(envelope.Event);
        return writer.ApplyAsync(card, RegistrationCards.Source(envelope, card), CancellationToken.None);
    }
}

 The writer recognizes consumer/stream/version identities and commits its receipt with the view changes. It handles duplicates, source conflicts, and ordering according to the events it consumes. An attribute does not create a database schema or durable recovery policy. 
 A compatible event-store route can persist projection handoffs with events. The host operates the consumers and recovery workers. If a projection fails after a known event commit, repair its pending read-side work rather than appending the business command again. 
 A small immutable read model 
 The same event facts can be folded into an immutable model.  RegistrationDetails  implements its view calculation with  On  methods returning another record. 
 public sealed record RegistrationDetails(Guid Id) : TroolioReadModel
{
    public Guid OwnerId { get; init; }
    public Guid WorkshopId { get; init; }
    public string DisplayName { get; init; } = "";
    public string Status { get; init; } = "absent";

    public RegistrationDetails On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationRequested> envelope) =>
        this with { OwnerId = envelope.Event.AttendeeId, WorkshopId = envelope.Event.WorkshopId,
            DisplayName = envelope.Event.DisplayName, Status = "requested" };

    public RegistrationDetails On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationCancelled> _) =>
        this with { Status = "cancelled" };

    public override Func<Metadata, bool> Authorized =>
        headers => OwnerId != Guid.Empty && headers.UserId == OwnerId;
}

 TroolioReadModel  is one query-side building block.  ReadModelProjectionActor<T>  can record links to selected source events, and  ReadModelResolver<T>  folds the model's stream using a GUID constructor and event handlers. The store must resolve links correctly. 
 This exact-model read is different from an indexed SQL catalogue. A growing linked history can make repeated reads expensive. Use a durable semantic projection for frequently queried cross-actor lists, schedules, reports, and notification feeds. 
 Authorization and freshness 
 The model's  Authorized  property expresses an access rule; the application must enforce it using the current authenticated caller. The base implementation defaults true, and the generic resolver alone is not an access-control boundary. 
 A successful command can precede the projection update. Define whether a query returns a current result, a known stale result, or a not-ready status. A UI can show accepted command progress and refresh after a committed-data hint. Source positions are meaningful within their source contract; there is no automatic total order across all actors. 
 Continue with  projection design ,  read-model design ,  idempotency and ordering , and  realtime updates . The  workshop walkthrough  connects the roles with downloadable C# source.

Actor boundaries and identity 
 An actor groups state and the commands that decide changes to it. Start by asking which facts must be considered together for one decision. A registration actor owns a particular registration. A workshop roster actor owns the roster for a particular workshop. These are separate identities even if the UI shows both on one page. On this page Choose a stable actor key Put the rule where its facts live Creation and lifecycle Design for the actor's history Scheduling and concurrency 
 A useful boundary follows a domain responsibility, rather than a controller method or database table. It should be possible to explain what the actor owns, how it is addressed, and which rules it enforces. 
 Choose a stable actor key 
 The workshop sample addresses a registration by a GUID. The same key is used in its domain message so the actor can reject a mismatch. 
 public static Task RequestAsync(ITroolioClient client, Guid registrationId, Guid workshopId,
    string displayName, Guid authenticatedUserId, Guid verifiedDeviceId, Guid correlationId)
{
    Metadata headers = MessageContext.AtIngress(correlationId, authenticatedUserId, verifiedDeviceId);
    return client.Tell<IRegistrationActor>(registrationId.ToString("D"),
        new RequestRegistration(headers, registrationId, workshopId, displayName));
}

 The  IRegistrationActor  contract identifies the actor role, and the key identifies one instance of that role. A user ID, registration ID, workshop ID, and source stream name are separate identifiers. Use explicit names in messages instead of assuming every GUID means the same entity. 
 Put the rule where its facts live 
 The registration actor decides whether that registration already exists and whether its owner can cancel it. The roster actor records the registration's participation in a workshop. A read model shows those outcomes efficiently to readers. 
 If the application introduces a hard capacity rule, a single authoritative actor or commit policy must own the capacity decision. Checking a projected count and then requesting a place can race with another registration. The projection is a convenient view; the capacity decision needs current write-side state and the chosen concurrency contract. 
 The sample models registration requests and roster bookkeeping. It does not claim to reserve a finite seat across two independent actors. For a multi-participant invariant, review  atomic commit  and the selected route's full store and authority requirements. 
 Creation and lifecycle 
 An actor reference can exist before the domain entity does. Decide how the first command creates state and what other commands do while it is absent. The sample's state starts with status  absent ; only a valid request transitions it to  requested . 
 [GenerateSerializer]
public record RegistrationState(Guid RegistrationId, Guid WorkshopId,
    Guid AttendeeId, string DisplayName, string Status) : IActorState
{
    public static RegistrationState Empty => new(Guid.Empty, Guid.Empty,
        Guid.Empty, "", "absent");

    public RegistrationState Apply(RegistrationRequested e) =>
        new(e.RegistrationId, e.WorkshopId, e.AttendeeId, e.DisplayName, "requested");

    public RegistrationState Apply(RegistrationCancelled e) =>
        new(e.RegistrationId, e.WorkshopId, e.AttendeeId, e.DisplayName, "cancelled");
}

 CreatableActor  provides an explicit creation lifecycle when that base class suits the model.  EventSourcedActor  can also implement domain creation rules directly, as this example does. Whichever approach you use, test duplicate creation, cancellation, and reuse of the key after deletion or retirement. 
 Design for the actor's history 
 An event-sourced actor loads its own history on activation and applies events to rebuild current state. Keep a boundary narrow enough that one actor's state and history remain manageable. A global actor that accumulates every customer and registration creates a large coordination and replay bottleneck. 
 For a catalogue across many actors, build a  read model  from committed events. Avoid activating every registration actor just to render a list. Queries that must examine many records belong to an indexed query model with bounded reads. 
 Scheduling and concurrency 
 Orleans controls activation and scheduling, but interleaving attributes change how requests can overlap around asynchronous work. Adding  Reentrant  to an actor with mutable decision state requires a review of its invariants. The sample leaves the state-owning actors without that attribute. 
 Even with orderly processing in one actor, calls to different actors are not automatically one database transaction. Stream expected versions and provider fences still matter. See  Orleans scheduling  for the runtime model, then  orchestration workflows  for cross-actor work. 
 Continue with  CQRS in practice ,  replay and live events , and the  workshop walkthrough .

Orchestration workflows 
 Orchestration connects domain decisions. An event from one actor can cause a command to another actor whose state owns the next decision. A registration request can add a registration ID to a workshop roster; a cancellation can remove it. On this page A typed event-to-command listener Give the target a safe duplicate policy Decide which workflow protocol is required Long-running workflows Observe the whole chain 
 Keep that relationship explicit: which event is the trigger, which actor is the destination, and which command describes the next action? It is easier to understand and recover than embedding unrelated writes inside the source actor's replay handler. 
 A typed event-to-command listener 
 The listener uses  OrchestrationStreamSubscription  to observe the registration actor's events. Its  On  methods address the roster actor using the workshop ID in the payload. 
 [OrchestrationStreamSubscription(typeof(RegistrationActor))]
public sealed class WorkshopEnrolmentOrchestration : OrchestrationActor
{
    public Task On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationRequested> envelope) =>
        System.ActorOf<IWorkshopRosterActor>(envelope.Event.WorkshopId.ToString("D"))
            .Tell(new AddAttendee(envelope.Event.Headers, envelope.Event.WorkshopId,
                envelope.Event.RegistrationId, envelope.Event.AttendeeId));

    public Task On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationCancelled> envelope) =>
        System.ActorOf<IWorkshopRosterActor>(envelope.Event.WorkshopId.ToString("D"))
            .Tell(new RemoveAttendee(envelope.Event.Headers, envelope.Event.WorkshopId,
                envelope.Event.RegistrationId));
}

 The event's headers are passed to  InternalCommand . Ordinary Troolio dispatch derives the next command's message and causation context. The business registration ID remains in the payload so the destination can recognize repeated work independently of diagnostics. 
 Return or await the command's task. Starting a task and returning before it completes makes failure and completion harder to associate with the trigger. A detached task also needs a durable job record if it must survive process loss. An in-memory task is not such a record. 
 Give the target a safe duplicate policy 
 A repeated  AddAttendee  should not inflate the count. The roster records registration IDs and checks whether an ID already maps to the same attendee. A conflicting reuse of that ID is an error. 
 Cancellation needs more than removing a dictionary entry. A cancellation may be delivered before an older add, or the add may be retried later. The sample records removed registration IDs as tombstones, so cancelled registrations remain cancelled. 
 public IEnumerable<Event> Handle(RemoveAttendee command)
{
    if (!Guid.TryParse(Id, out Guid workshopId) || workshopId != command.WorkshopId)
        throw new ArgumentException("Workshop key mismatch.");
    return removed.Contains(command.RegistrationId)
        ? [] : [new AttendeeRemoved(command.Headers, command.RegistrationId)];
}

public void On(AttendeeAdded e) => attendees = attendees.Add(e.RegistrationId, e.AttendeeId);
public void On(AttendeeRemoved e)
{
    attendees = attendees.Remove(e.RegistrationId);
    removed = removed.Add(e.RegistrationId);
}

 Tombstones themselves have a retention policy. If they are discarded while old work can still arrive, the application needs another durable identity or lifecycle fence. A large roster may also need a different boundary rather than one actor that retains unlimited registration history. 
 Decide which workflow protocol is required 



 Need 
 Design choice 




 React to one actor event with another domain command 
 An ordinary orchestration listener plus the target's validation and retry policy. 


 Commit a reviewed closed set of participant events and handoffs together 
 Selected event-only planning, route authority, and a capable atomic store. 


 Wait for an external provider or human decision 
 Durable application workflow state and effect/job workers. 


 Build a list or report for readers 
 A projection and read model. 



 Ordinary orchestration uses Troolio's command transaction context, but a listener by itself does not establish a provider's atomic multi-stream guarantee. The  selected route  requires reviewed handlers, closed planning, and store capabilities. The full operation must be checked at that boundary. 
 Long-running workflows 
 A provider call can take seconds or minutes and may have an uncertain outcome. Record the work to be done, dispatch it through an idempotent provider, and then record the known result as another domain command. Avoid keeping one actor request open indefinitely while waiting for a remote system or an approval click. 
 A useful state model is  requested → processing → succeeded  or  requested → processing → failed . Preserve a workflow or attempt ID in each result so a late response cannot complete a newer attempt. A cancelled workflow should define what happens to an already-running provider request; cancellation does not roll back a remote action automatically. 
 If an external step already happened, a compensating action is another explicit business operation, such as cancelling a provider booking. It has its own outcome and retry policy. Do not describe it as undoing an event history. 
 Observe the whole chain 
 Log the triggering stream/version, correlation, causation, business workflow ID, destination actor, and attempt outcome. Keep private payloads out of routine logs. Use separate statuses for rejected commands, transient failure, and uncertain provider results. 
 Continue with  correlation and causation ,  notifications and background work , and  idempotency and ordering . The complete original listener is in the  workshop example .

Design a durable projection 
 A projection translates domain events into the facts needed by readers. The workshop application projects registration events into a small SQL card containing registration ID, workshop ID, attendee ID, display name, and status. That lets a UI read one record without restoring the actor. On this page Map the event into a view Use semantic tables Commit the view and source receipt together Keep the handoff durable Make failure visible at the right scope 
 Begin with the query: what does the page need, how does it filter, who can access it, and how many records may one request return? Those answers define the table and indexes more usefully than serializing the actor's entire state into a generic row. 

 Map the event into a view 
 A typed listener makes the supported inputs visible. The sample handles request and cancellation events and maps each to a complete registration card. 
 [ProjectionStreamSubscription(typeof(RegistrationActor))]
public sealed class RegistrationCardsProjection(IRegistrationCardWriter writer) : ProjectionActor
{
    public Task On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationRequested> envelope)
    {
        RegistrationCard card = RegistrationCards.From(envelope.Event);
        return writer.ApplyAsync(card, RegistrationCards.Source(envelope, card), CancellationToken.None);
    }

    public Task On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationCancelled> envelope)
    {
        RegistrationCard card = RegistrationCards.From(envelope.Event);
        return writer.ApplyAsync(card, RegistrationCards.Source(envelope, card), CancellationToken.None);
    }
}

 IRegistrationCardWriter  is an application contract. The host registers a durable implementation, operates the projection route and recovery, and supplies access to the verified committed source. The attribute declares a subscription; it does not create a SQL table, credential, or replay worker. 
 The sample verifies that the envelope's source actor ID matches the registration ID and records stream/version as its source identity. It hashes the domain card separately from trace headers for stable comparison. A hash comparison is not authentication of an event: the serving route still owns trusted source admission. 
 Use semantic tables 
 The example schema has typed columns and an index on attendee, workshop, and registration ID. A user's registration list can therefore filter in SQL and return a small page. A notification view or reporting view may need another table and another index because it answers a different question. 
 A whole-actor JSON row can be helpful for diagnostics or migration. As a normal cross-actor query path, it often forces the application to load, parse, and scan too much data. Promote frequently used fields into purpose-built columns and keep queries bounded. 
 Commit the view and source receipt together 
 The SQL sample uses one transaction for the materialized card and its projection inbox record. The inbox key is consumer, source stream, and source version. A retry with a retained identical identity returns a duplicate outcome; a changed domain payload at that identity fails. 
 CREATE TABLE dbo.WorkshopProjectionInbox
(
    Consumer nvarchar(64) NOT NULL,
    SourceStream nvarchar(256) NOT NULL,
    SourceVersion bigint NOT NULL,
    DomainHash char(64) NOT NULL,
    CONSTRAINT PK_WorkshopProjectionInbox
        PRIMARY KEY (Consumer, SourceStream, SourceVersion)
);

 The complete writer and schema are in the  workshop example . It is an application projection for full-state cards. It does not implement the framework's atomic event transaction store or a complete migration/deployment policy. 
 Delta projections require a different rule: a balance increment or per-event counter cannot simply skip an older unapplied event. Preserve ordering, detect relevant gaps, and recover missing source work before advancing. See  idempotency and ordering . 
 Keep the handoff durable 
 An event commit and a projection write are separate durability boundaries. A compatible store can commit projection handoff work with events, and a recovery worker can redeliver it after a crash. Choose and enable that route explicitly. Inline publication and a subscription alone do not provide the same recovery record. 
 If SQL fails after an event was committed, the event still belongs to history. Restore the pending projection work and retry its writer; do not pretend the command never happened or append it again to make the view catch up. 
 Make failure visible at the right scope 
 A missing source, unknown event type, or invalid payload is different from a temporary SQL timeout. Repeated transient failures need retry/backoff. Deterministically invalid data needs a quarantined record and operator action, with enough source identity to investigate. 
 One broken registration should not require global startup to replay every actor. Keep repair bounded and isolate affected streams or partitions. Report projection readiness and lag where the read endpoint can use them. 
 Continue with  read-model design ,  catch-up and rebuilds , and  realtime updates .

Design read models 
 A read model presents information for a particular reader. It can combine facts from several actors, omit write-side details, and organize fields for a UI or report. It is derived from accepted history, while the write-side decision remains with the responsible actor or commit policy. On this page A pure immutable model How the Troolio resolver works Map a source into the linked model Enforce authorization explicitly SQL serving models Shape the model for the task 
 The term covers several implementations. Troolio supplies  TroolioReadModel  and a resolver that folds an event stream. A larger application can also maintain semantic SQL tables through durable projections. Choose deliberately: the cost, freshness, and access boundary differ. 
 A pure immutable model 
 The example's  RegistrationDetails  has a  Guid  constructor, fields for a registration detail view, and  On  methods that return a new record. 
 public sealed record RegistrationDetails(Guid Id) : TroolioReadModel
{
    public Guid OwnerId { get; init; }
    public Guid WorkshopId { get; init; }
    public string DisplayName { get; init; } = "";
    public string Status { get; init; } = "absent";

    public RegistrationDetails On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationRequested> envelope) =>
        this with { OwnerId = envelope.Event.AttendeeId, WorkshopId = envelope.Event.WorkshopId,
            DisplayName = envelope.Event.DisplayName, Status = "requested" };

    public RegistrationDetails On(EventEnvelope<RegistrationCancelled> _) =>
        this with { Status = "cancelled" };

    public override Func<Metadata, bool> Authorized =>
        headers => OwnerId != Guid.Empty && headers.UserId == OwnerId;
}

 The  with  expression preserves the previous record and changes the relevant fields. It does not perform a database read or call another actor. That makes the calculation useful for deterministic event folding and for testing historical sequences. 
 A record does not make everything deeply immutable. Arrays, lists, and mutable objects inside a record can still change. Use immutable collections or copy mutable input before retaining it as model state. 
 How the Troolio resolver works 
 ITroolioClient.Get<TReadModel>(id)  asks  ReadModelResolver<TReadModel>  to construct the model using the parsed GUID ID and apply the events in its read-model stream.  ReadModelProjectionActor<T>  can map source envelopes to a model key and record links; the chosen  IStore  must resolve those link records correctly. 
 This is useful for a small exact model with a known event history. It is not automatically an indexed SQL catalogue, and fetching one model can fold its linked history again. Monitor the cost of that read as history grows. 
 The resolver's local folding envelope versions should not be mistaken for the authoritative source revision of a multi-stream projection. If the UI needs an explicit freshness contract, persist the relevant source positions or operation receipt in the application read model. 
 Map a source into the linked model 
 The optional linker selects the registration ID as the destination model key: 
 [ProjectionStreamSubscription(typeof(RegistrationActor))]
public sealed class RegistrationDetailsLinks(IStore store)
    : ReadModelProjectionActor<RegistrationDetails>(store)
{
    public string Handle(EventEnvelope<RegistrationRequested> envelope) =>
        envelope.Event.RegistrationId.ToString("D");

    public string Handle(EventEnvelope<RegistrationCancelled> envelope) =>
        envelope.Event.RegistrationId.ToString("D");
}

 This linked fold assumes an ordered relevant history. The model's pure methods do not supply durable delivery deduplication or protect against out-of-order link appends by themselves. The host must provide that source contract. For the worked application's normal SQL reads, use  RegistrationCardsProjection  and its inbox writer instead. 
 Enforce authorization explicitly 
 TroolioReadModel.Authorized  defaults to allowing access. An override expresses a model rule, but the host must invoke and enforce it with headers constructed from the current authenticated caller. The generic client resolver does not establish that security boundary for you. 
 public static async Task<RegistrationDetails> ReadDetailsAsync(ITroolioClient client,
    Guid registrationId, Metadata verifiedCaller)
{
    RegistrationDetails details = await client.Get<RegistrationDetails>(registrationId.ToString("D"));
    if (!details.Authorized(verifiedCaller)) throw new UnauthorizedAccessException();
    return details;
}

 Do not run this check against user IDs copied from an untrusted request body. Do not cache an authorized result indefinitely after the user loses access. A staff reader and an attendee reader may need separate rules rather than one broad view. 
 SQL serving models 
 For lists, cross-actor catalogues, search, notifications, and reporting, a durable projection can maintain typed SQL rows. The sample's exact reader applies the attendee filter in the database: 
 public async Task<RegistrationCard?> GetForUserAsync(Guid registrationId, Guid authenticatedUserId,
    CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    await using var connection = new SqlConnection(connectionString);
    await connection.OpenAsync(cancellationToken);
    using var command = connection.CreateCommand();
    command.CommandText = """
        SELECT RegistrationId, WorkshopId, AttendeeId, DisplayName, Status
        FROM dbo.WorkshopRegistrationCard WHERE RegistrationId = @id AND AttendeeId = @user;
        """;
    command.Parameters.Add("@id", SqlDbType.UniqueIdentifier).Value = registrationId;
    command.Parameters.Add("@user", SqlDbType.UniqueIdentifier).Value = authenticatedUserId;
    await using SqlDataReader reader = await command.ExecuteReaderAsync(cancellationToken);
    return await reader.ReadAsync(cancellationToken)
        ? new(reader.GetGuid(0), reader.GetGuid(1), reader.GetGuid(2), reader.GetString(3), reader.GetString(4))
        : null;
}

 A page reader should also have a fixed maximum size and stable continuation cursor. Fetching every record and filtering in memory makes access control, memory use, and latency harder to reason about. Treat missing, unavailable, and not-yet-current projection outcomes distinctly where the API needs that information. 
 Shape the model for the task 
 A registration detail page needs one card. A roster page needs a list and maybe a count. A reminder worker needs an indexed due-time query. A reporting page may need historical aggregates. Keep those shapes explicit rather than making every caller reconstruct and interpret the full actor state. 
 A stale read model can explain a recent state to a user, but a business invariant still needs current write-side checks. A projected attendee count alone cannot allocate the final available place safely. 
 Continue with  CQRS in practice ,  projection design , and  realtime updates . See the  workshop sample  for both the immutable model and the SQL reader.

Idempotency and ordering 
 A distributed consumer can see the same work more than once. It may also see a later source before an earlier one, depending on the provider, subscriptions, and recovery path. Decide what identity establishes a duplicate and what ordering your calculation requires. On this page Three identities at three boundaries A projection source record Full-state snapshots and deltas need different rules Prevent late resurrection in an actor workflow Reconcile uncertain command outcomes Provider behavior still matters 
 “Use the same correlation ID” is not a duplicate rule. Many distinct messages in one workflow share that ID. Likewise,  EventEnvelope.Id  identifies an actor, so all its events can have the same value. 
 Three identities at three boundaries 



 Boundary 
 Identity to preserve 




 Command operation 
 Business request key and the selected route's verified operation/request identity. 


 Projection source 
 Consumer identity plus the provider's exact source stream and version/position. 


 External effect 
 Stable effect identity and the provider's durable idempotency key. 



 Use diagnostic message IDs to investigate processing. Trace paths can derive delivery headers, so those IDs are not a substitute for a stable persisted source locator. 
 A projection source record 
 The example retains the source identity alongside the domain comparison hash: 
 public sealed record RegistrationCard(Guid RegistrationId, Guid WorkshopId,
    Guid AttendeeId, string DisplayName, string Status);

public sealed record ProjectionSource(string Consumer, string Stream, ulong Version, string DomainHash);
public enum ApplyOutcome { Applied, Duplicate, OlderSnapshot }

public interface IRegistrationCardWriter
{
    Task<ApplyOutcome> ApplyAsync(RegistrationCard card, ProjectionSource source,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken);
}

 A SQL writer commits the inbox and view changes together. If it crashes before commit, neither is durable and the source can be retried. If it crashes after commit, the retained inbox lets the retry return the completed outcome. 
 Compare content when the same source identity is reused. A different payload at the same stream/version should not silently overwrite the view. The sample uses a domain-only hash so a diagnostic header change does not create a false content conflict. Source authorization and exact persisted-byte validation belong to the selected route/provider where required. 
 Full-state snapshots and deltas need different rules 
 The registration card events contain all fields needed to represent the current card. The writer can keep the newer snapshot and record an older input as processed without replacing the current row. 
 A delta event such as “add one attendee to the total” is different. Skipping an older delta loses a contribution. An ordered delta consumer needs its preceding checkpoint and a verified way to recover missing inputs. Do not apply a “largest version wins” strategy to every event type. 
 Version arithmetic also depends on the source contract. A subscriber may filter out events, and framework creation events may not be published to the same listener. A numeric gap is therefore not universally evidence of a missing domain event. Define what a consumer expects to receive from that provider. 
 Prevent late resurrection in an actor workflow 
 An actor that removes an entry from a set can accept an old add again if it has forgotten that removal. The roster sample retains removed registration IDs and treats their cancellation as terminal. 
 public IEnumerable<Event> Handle(RemoveAttendee command)
{
    if (!Guid.TryParse(Id, out Guid workshopId) || workshopId != command.WorkshopId)
        throw new ArgumentException("Workshop key mismatch.");
    return removed.Contains(command.RegistrationId)
        ? [] : [new AttendeeRemoved(command.Headers, command.RegistrationId)];
}

public void On(AttendeeAdded e) => attendees = attendees.Add(e.RegistrationId, e.AttendeeId);
public void On(AttendeeRemoved e)
{
    attendees = attendees.Remove(e.RegistrationId);
    removed = removed.Add(e.RegistrationId);
}

 The example does not reuse a registration ID after cancellation. If your domain supports a new attempt, model its attempt ID or generation explicitly. Retain duplicate and lifecycle records for as long as delayed work can still arrive. 
 Reconcile uncertain command outcomes 
 A timeout can happen after events committed. Query the authoritative operation or actor state before deciding whether another command is safe. A new operation ID can represent another business action, so inventing one on every retry can create duplicate work. 
 The  atomic commit  contract compares the original request and frozen batch on retries. Durable no-op completion and effect completion have separate contextual requirements. Do not infer these from an ordinary empty event list. 
 Provider behavior still matters 
 Orleans stream subscription operations and event delivery guarantees depend on the chosen provider and configured path. Read  the streaming documentation  together with the provider contract, and prove the application's consumer behavior under redelivery and process loss. 
 Continue with  recovery ,  projection catch-up , and  external effects .

Notifications and background work 
 Applications often turn an accepted domain event into something a person sees: an in-app notice, a reminder, or an email. Those outcomes have different durability and access requirements. Model them explicitly instead of putting all work into an event handler with a provider call. On this page Project notification facts Send through durable effect state Schedule later work with a new scope Plan the failure cases 
 A notification fact is data that a reader can query. A provider send is an external effect. A scheduled job is future work with its own execution identity. Rebuilding a read model or activating an actor should not accidentally repeat any of them. 
 Project notification facts 
 A notification projection can create one row per intended recipient and source event. Store the recipient, notification kind, source identity, current status, and the minimum content or reference required for the reader. 
 The sample helper creates a stable recipient-specific key from source stream/version and a versioned notification kind: 
 public static class NotificationIdentity
{
    public static string ForRecipient(ProjectionSource source, Guid recipientId)
    {
        string[] fields = ["registration-notification-v1", source.Stream,
            source.Version.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture), recipientId.ToString("D")];
        return Convert.ToHexStringLower(SHA256.HashData(JsonSerializer.SerializeToUtf8Bytes(fields)));
    }
}

 A production writer uses a unique constraint and commits this key with its notification row. The reader still authorizes the current recipient. If the row only references a registration, reread that registration through its current access boundary before exposing protected details. 
 The acting user and recipient need not match. An administrator may initiate a cancellation that notifies an attendee. Keep attribution in metadata and recipient identity in the domain notification record; do not overwrite one to represent the other. 
 Send through durable effect state 
 The framework's intent protocol requires a committed event receipt and an idempotent provider: 
 public static class ConfirmationEffects
{
    public static Task<ExternalEffectIntent> RegisterAsync(IExternalEffectIntentStore store,
        IIdempotentExternalEffectProvider provider, AtomicEventTransactionReceipt receipt, byte[] payload) =>
        ExternalEffectDispatcher.RegisterAsync(store, provider, receipt,
            "registration-confirmation", "confirmation-worker", payload);
}

 This helper applies when the application is using the compatible receipt/intent path. An ordinary projection that creates its own notification outbox needs its own transaction and worker contract; it should not manufacture an atomic event receipt. 
 A worker dispatches pending intents with the retained key and records a definitive terminal result. A timeout or uncertain result remains recoverable. If an application records an intent only after the event commit, it must handle the gap between those steps; a capable atomic event/effect store can commit them together under its stronger contract. 
 Schedule later work with a new scope 
 A reminder tomorrow runs after the original command's transaction has ended. Keep the durable reminder/job identity and causal trigger, then construct current execution context when it runs: 
 public static Metadata Scheduled(Metadata trigger, Guid currentUserId) =>
    new(Guid.NewGuid(), currentUserId, Guid.Empty)
    {
        CausationId = trigger.MessageId,
        TransactionId = null
    };

 This example chooses a new correlation for the later execution and preserves a causal reference. Recheck user or service authority at execution time. A completed transaction ID should not be reused as if it were still active. 
 An in-memory timer is useful for waking local work, but the work's pending state must be durable if it must survive process loss. Orleans reminders and the Troolio desired-state effect provider have their own configuration and host capability requirements. See  external effects  and  reliable jobs . 
 Plan the failure cases 
 If a confirmation was sent but the worker crashed before recording its receipt, a retry should use the same provider key. If access was revoked before the send, current policy may suppress it or produce a definitive superseded outcome. If a recipient's view is rebuilt from old events, its retained notification identity should prevent duplicate facts or sends. 
 Rate limits, retry backoff, maximum attempt age, dead-letter outcomes, and provider credentials belong to the application worker. Monitor pending age and definitive outcomes, and provide a way to investigate one source without replaying every user's history. 
 Continue with  orchestration workflows ,  idempotency and ordering , and  observability .

Atomic event commit 
 AtomicEventTransaction.CommitAsync  accepts a complete  AtomicEventTransactionRequest . Each participant names an actor path, stream, expected version, and nonempty event list. The request carries one operation ID, a lowercase SHA-256 request identity, and an optional  AtomicRouteFence . On this page Required store behavior No-change outcomes Example: Commit a closed event batch 
 Required store behavior 
 The store must implement  IAtomicEventTransactionStore  and advertise  SupportsAtomicEventTransactionsAndProjectionDispatch . It commits  all  participant events, required ancillary streams, projection dispatch handoffs, and the keyed completion receipt as one atomic operation. A selected fenced route also requires  IRouteFencedAtomicEventTransactionStore  and a commit-time comparison of route key, epoch, and deployment fingerprint. 
 Before returning a prior receipt on retry, the store compares the full frozen serialized batch. Reusing an operation ID with changed actor paths, expected versions, event types, or bytes must fail without another append. Current ownership, authorization, and deletion fences still apply to a retry. 
 AtomicEventTransactionReceipt  records committed versions and attests to ancillary writes and projection handoffs. The helper validates that the receipt matches the request and expected final versions. External effects are outside this contract; read  external effects  for the durable intent boundary. 
 No-change outcomes 
 The regular eventful contract rejects an empty participant or empty event list. A durable no-op completion is a separate optional contextual capability with its own receipt shape and root-state checks. Do not manufacture an eventful receipt to represent a no-op. 
 Example: Commit a closed event batch 
 Given a capable store and the verified identity from the  selected command route , this commits one participant at expected version zero. The current route fence is passed into the request and checked by the store. 
 public static Task<AtomicEventTransactionReceipt> Commit(
    IStore store, Metadata headers, TrustedAtomicCommandIdentityClaim identity,
    AtomicRouteFence routeFence)
{
    var participant = new AtomicEventTransactionParticipant(
        "CounterActor/42", "CounterActor-42", 0,
        new IEvent[] { new Incremented(headers) });
    var request = new AtomicEventTransactionRequest(
        identity.OperationId, identity.RequestIdentity, [participant], routeFence);
    return AtomicEventTransaction.CommitAsync(store, request);
}

Tracing and observability 
 A useful diagnostic record connects the business interaction to the processing step that failed or became slow. Troolio metadata provides correlation, causation, and transaction context. The event provider contributes stream and source position. Application logging and .NET tracing can make those facts searchable and measurable. On this page Use structured scopes and spans Follow the causal chain Protect the diagnostic surface Measure the work after commit Add checks at the failure boundary 
 Keep business identity alongside diagnostics. “Registration 123 was requested twice” and “this dispatch took two seconds” are different questions, and one trace ID does not answer both automatically. 
 Use structured scopes and spans 
 The example instruments application-owned work with an  ILogger  scope and an  ActivitySource  span. Its callback supplies the operation being observed. 
 public static class WorkflowTelemetry
{
    public static readonly ActivitySource Source = new("Workshop.Workflows");

    public static async Task ExecuteAsync(ILogger logger, Metadata headers, Func<Task> work)
    {
        using IDisposable? scope = logger.BeginScope(new Dictionary<string, object?>
        {
            ["CorrelationId"] = headers.CorrelationId,
            ["MessageId"] = headers.MessageId,
            ["CausationId"] = headers.CausationId,
            ["TransactionId"] = headers.TransactionId
        });
        using Activity? activity = Source.StartActivity("registration.process", ActivityKind.Internal);
        activity?.SetTag("troolio.correlation_id", headers.CorrelationId.ToString("D"));
        try
        {
            await work();
            logger.LogInformation("Registration processing completed");
        }
        catch (Exception error)
        {
            activity?.SetStatus(ActivityStatusCode.Error, error.GetType().Name);
            logger.LogError(error, "Registration processing failed");
            throw;
        }
    }
}

 StartActivity  may return null when no listener requests a span, so instrumentation should tolerate that. Register an appropriate telemetry listener/exporter in your host to collect activities. The scope keeps related IDs available in logs even when trace collection is not enabled. 
 The  official .NET instrumentation guide  describes  ActivitySource  and  Activity . A Troolio correlation GUID is an application tag; it does not automatically create a W3C parent context or propagate  traceparent  across remote calls. Preserve that trace context separately when the transport supports it. 
 Follow the causal chain 
 Begin an investigation with the business operation and correlation ID. Find the handled command, emitted events, target orchestration commands, projection work, and effect outcomes. Causation identifies the direct parent when a workflow branches. 
 For a projection failure, also retain consumer name, source stream/version, and projection version. For a selected command, retain the verified operation identity and route epoch. For an external effect, retain intent identity and provider receipt. Log the outcome category so a transient failure can be distinguished from invalid data or an uncertain provider result. 
 Protect the diagnostic surface 
 IDs can still identify people or devices. Restrict log access and retention. Avoid logging private event bodies, connection credentials, prompts, or provider tokens just to correlate messages. Error messages from providers may also contain sensitive details, so shape public errors independently from operator diagnostics. 
 Do not use arbitrary user, actor, correlation, or message IDs as metric labels. Their cardinality can grow with every request. Metrics can use bounded dimensions such as consumer kind, route, status category, and deployment version, while logs carry the individual identifiers needed for an investigation. 
 Measure the work after commit 
 Useful signals include command latency and conflicts, projection apply duration, oldest pending handoff, catch-up progress, quarantined sources, pending effect age, and provider terminal outcomes. A successful command dashboard alone misses a read side that has been stuck for hours. 
 Measure per meaningful partition where it helps isolate impact. A global ready signal should not conceal one unavailable user's view, and one broken partition should not automatically mark unrelated application reads unavailable. 
 Add checks at the failure boundary 
 Exercise process loss before and after event commit, projection commit, source acknowledgement, and provider execution. Inspect whether the retained IDs and statuses let you determine the outcome. A timeout with no operation identity in diagnostics leaves the application unable to decide whether a retry is safe. 
 Continue with  message metadata ,  correlation and causation , and  projection catch-up .

Run the Calculator sample 
 The smallest runnable Troolio example: send two commands, record events, and query an actor's sum. On this page Run Follow the code Get the complete source 
 Run 
 Install the .NET 10 SDK, then run from the repository root: 
 dotnet run --project Sample/Calculator

 The program prints  42 + 19 = 61  and exits without requiring keyboard input. Run one default local silo at a time; its Orleans ports are 9000 and 9001. 
 Follow the code 
 var headers = new Metadata(Guid.NewGuid(), Guid.NewGuid(), Guid.NewGuid());
await client.Tell(actorId, new RecordInteger(headers, 42));
var sum = await client.Ask(actorId, new Sum());

 The correlation ID groups related work, the user ID identifies the caller already established by your application, and the device ID identifies its client. This console sample creates these IDs locally; they are not an authentication system. The runtime assigns message lineage as it handles each command and event. 
 public IEnumerable<Event> Handle(RecordInteger command)
    => [new IntegerRecorded(command.Headers, command.Value)];
public void On(IntegerRecorded e)
    => State = State with { Total = checked(State.Total + e.Value) };

 Handle  decides what happened.  On  restores only the actor's own state and can be repeated during replay without outside effects. This sample's default store is in memory: restart loses its events. The  ToDo sample  adds relational read models and durable store choices. The  Shopping List sample  adds collaboration and orchestration. 
